---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

// Real walk-forward backtest output — wnba-bet/analysis/backtest.py, 2024 season.
const calibrationPoints = [
  { cx: 66.5, cy: 137.3, r: 6.9 },
  { cx: 99.5, cy: 177.1, r: 7.6 },
  { cx: 132.5, cy: 148.3, r: 7.9 },
  { cx: 165.5, cy: 175.8, r: 7.9 },
  { cx: 198.5, cy: 166.7, r: 7.9 },
  { cx: 231.5, cy: 93.3, r: 7.9 },
  { cx: 264.5, cy: 114.3, r: 8.3 },
  { cx: 297.5, cy: 98.5, r: 8.3 },
  { cx: 330.5, cy: 91.7, r: 9.6 },
  { cx: 363.5, cy: 20, r: 6.0 },
];
---

<Layout
	title="Sports Forecasting & Market-Pricing Engine — Rahul Raja Durai Murugan"
	description="Probabilistic forecasting models compared against real market prices, with calibration fit from live data instead of guessed. A personal research project, not a promotional betting tool."
	current="/projects"
>
	<article class="project sans">
		<header class="project-head">
			<p class="eyebrow">Project · Probabilistic forecasting</p>
			<h1>Sports Forecasting &amp; Market-Pricing Engine</h1>
			<p class="dek">Probability models for game outcomes and player props, compared continuously against real market prices — a personal research project in calibration and pricing, not a betting service.</p>
			<ul class="tags mono">
				<li>Python</li><li>XGBoost</li><li>Discord.py</li><li>The Odds API</li><li>SQLite</li>
			</ul>
		</header>

		<section>
			<h2>Problem</h2>
			<p>Betting markets are priced by professionals and move in real time, so a genuine mispricing between two books is rare and short-lived — finding one means pulling live prices from multiple sources, converting between very different pricing conventions (pick'em platforms, sportsbook American odds, implied probability), and doing it continuously enough that a real gap doesn't disappear before anyone sees it. The forecasting problem underneath — assigning a calibrated probability to an uncertain outcome, then checking that probability against someone else's price — is the same one that shows up in demand forecasting or risk pricing; sports markets just happen to score you daily.</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Approach</h2>
			<p><b>One reference price, never a pick source.</b> FanDuel's price is pulled and de-vigged into a fair probability — used only as the reference line, never as a source of picks itself. Candidate picks come from two other places: Underdog's pick'em lines and DraftKings' own sportsbook price. The fair probability gets shifted from FanDuel's line to the pick's own line with a normal-distribution assumption, using a per-stat standard deviation.</p>

			<p><b>Calibrated against real data, not guessed.</b> Those standard deviations started as hand-guessed defaults and were meaningfully wrong in places — MLB total bases was guessed at 1.1, the value regression-fit from real alternate-line market data came out to 2.69. A separate script re-fits them from live odds whenever a new sport comes into season; the calibrated values are pasted in by hand rather than auto-applied, so a real vs. guessed constant is never silently swapped without a look.</p>

			<p><b>A genuinely independent second opinion.</b> Alongside the market-pricing side, a separate statistical model — season and recent-form blended rate, opponent defense/pace adjustment — computes its own probability from real ESPN game logs and reports whether it agrees or disagrees with the market-derived pick. It's a full independent port, not a shared import, and if a player can't be confidently matched to a team it reports "N/A" rather than guessing.</p>

			<div class="chart-card">
				<div class="chart-chrome">
					<div class="chart-head">
						<p class="chart-title"><span class="predicted-label">Predicted</span> vs. observed win rate</p>
						<p class="chart-meta mono">WNBA game model · 2024 season, n=240</p>
					</div>
					<p class="chart-caption">Walk-forward backtest — trained on 2022–2023 only, tested on 2024, no lookahead. Real output from <span class="mono">wnba-bet/analysis/backtest.py</span>, run against the live database.</p>
					<div class="chart-stats mono">
						<div class="stat"><p class="v">65.8%</p><p class="k sans">accuracy</p></div>
						<div class="stat"><p class="v">0.242</p><p class="k sans">Brier score</p></div>
						<div class="stat"><p class="v">240</p><p class="k sans">games</p></div>
					</div>
					<p class="chart-context"><b>What does this mean?</b> Sports markets are priced by professionals and move in real time, so consistently beating them is hard by design (a model that looked too good would be the actual red flag). A Brier score of 0.242 clears the 0.25 you'd get from pure guessing, and serious forecasting in any genuinely uncertain domain — weather, elections, sports — typically lands in this same 0.20–0.24 band even when the model is strong. The clearer signal: 65.8% accuracy against the 58% you'd get by always picking the home team is a real, repeatable edge, not noise.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="plot-panel">
					<div class="legend">
						<span class="legend-item"><span class="legend-dot"></span>observed rate</span>
						<span class="legend-item"><span class="legend-line"></span>perfect calibration</span>
					</div>
					<svg width="100%" height="220" viewBox="0 0 400 260">
						<line x1="50" y1="240" x2="50" y2="20" stroke="#D9D3D6" stroke-width="1" />
						<line x1="50" y1="240" x2="380" y2="240" stroke="#D9D3D6" stroke-width="1" />
						<line x1="50" y1="240" x2="380" y2="20" stroke="var(--wine)" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="4 4" />
						<text x="50" y="252" font-size="9" fill="#6B6570" font-family="monospace">0%</text>
						<text x="365" y="252" font-size="9" fill="#6B6570" font-family="monospace">100%</text>
						<text x="150" y="14" font-size="9.5" fill="#6B6570" font-family="sans-serif">predicted probability →</text>
						{calibrationPoints.map((p) => <circle cx={p.cx} cy={p.cy} r={p.r} fill="var(--periwinkle)" />)}
					</svg>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p><b>It always says something.</b> The Discord posting scheduler runs on a fixed 17-slot daily cadence and always posts at every slot — even when there's nothing new — a deliberate choice so silence in the channel never has to be interpreted as "quiet market" versus "bot is down." Slot state and per-pick dedup both persist in SQLite, so a host restart mid-window doesn't cause a duplicate post.</p>

			<p><b>A real incident shaped the safety design.</b> Early in development, a sportsbook API's events endpoint once returned an entire season — 272 NFL games — in one call, which would have cost 1,600+ of a limited monthly credit budget before an event-window filter existed. The fix wasn't just the filter: there's now a standing credit-safety check that refuses to spend anything if a single scan's estimated cost is too high, or if proceeding would drop reserves below a floor — checked for free, since the request that estimates cost doesn't itself cost credits.</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Result</h2>
			<div class="stat-row mono">
				<div class="stat"><p class="v">65.8%</p><p class="k sans">WNBA accuracy, 2024 backtest</p></div>
				<div class="stat"><p class="v">~38%</p><p class="k sans">of monthly API budget used</p></div>
				<div class="stat"><p class="v">17</p><p class="k sans">scheduled slots / day</p></div>
			</div>
			<p>Deployed and running live, not a local script — hosted with a keep-alive endpoint to work around the host's free-tier sleep policy, since a Discord bot otherwise receives no inbound traffic to stay awake on its own. Measured live cost for WNBA combo props runs to roughly 255 credits a day against a 20,000/month plan, with headroom to spare.</p>
			<p class="note">Personal research project. The "EV%" shown in the actual tool is a shifted win probability, not a dollar-return calculation, and it's labeled that way deliberately — not a profit guarantee.</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>What I'd change</h2>
			<p>Right now only WNBA combo props are active — NBA and NFL are fully wired into the codebase (the pricing math and market config both already support them) but sit dormant, since running multiple sports at once would multiply API credit usage faster than the current budget comfortably absorbs. Turning them back on is a one-line config change, not a rebuild, but it needs either a higher API tier or a lower posting cadence to stay within budget — I'd rather ship that deliberately than flip it on and find out the hard way mid-month.</p>
		</section>

		<section class="links">
			<h2>Links</h2>
			<ul>
				<li><a href="https://github.com/rahulrajadm/sports-betting-analytics-suite" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/rahulrajadm/sports-betting-analytics-suite</a></li>
				<li><a href="https://rahulrajadm.github.io/sports-betting-analytics-suite" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">rahulrajadm.github.io/sports-betting-analytics-suite</a></li>
			</ul>
		</section>
	</article>
</Layout>

